<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=saw">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Tools and materials</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saw</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 3100 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A saw is a tool with a tough blade, wire or chain bearing a hard toothed edge, used to cut wood, metal or stone. Saws began as serrated flint, obsidian, shells and shark teeth. In ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> open pull saws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> are documented as early as the Early Dynastic Period, c. 3100 to 2686 BCE, and many were found in a tomb dating to the reign of Djer. Egyptian saws later had teeth raked to cut on the pull stroke, and were also made of bronze and iron. Frame saws holding thin blades in tension were developed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, and the earliest known sawmill is the Roman Hierapolis sawmill of the third century CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saw.jpg" alt="Saw" width="330" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mnemosine · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pily_rozplatnice.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 3100 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Egypt</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Serrated tools showing signs of cutting wood from Pech-de-l'Azé cave IV in France are dated 90,000 to 30,000 years BCE; Chinese legend credits Lu Ban and Greek myth credits Talos.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">Stone tools<span>3.3 million years ago</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/copper-smelting">Copper smelting<span>c. 5000 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=saw">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
